---
import GameLayout from "../../../layouts/GameLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";

const snippetDir = path.join(process.cwd(), "src", "snippets", "drifter");

const readSyscallSnippet = fs
  .readFileSync(path.join(snippetDir, "read_syscall.txt"), "utf-8")
  .trim();

const drifter0Snippet = fs
  .readFileSync(path.join(snippetDir, "drifter0.c"), "utf-8")
  .trim();

const rc4Snippet = fs
  .readFileSync(path.join(snippetDir, "rc4.c"), "utf-8")
  .trim();

const rc4hSnippet = fs
  .readFileSync(path.join(snippetDir, "rc4.h"), "utf-8")
  .trim();
---

<GameLayout title="OverTheWire: Drifter Level 0" gameName="drifter" level={0}>
  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    Drifter can be accessed on drifter.labs.overthewire.org via SSH on port
    2230. Level 0 listens on port 1111.
  </p>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    Level0 is an extremely trivial, encrypted, remote syscall proxy. Your aim is
    to read the contents of a file called "drifter0.password" to get the
    password for user drifter0.
  </p>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    Upon connection, it sets up an encrypted rc4 key (based on the connecting IP
    address / port), read()'s in 9 integers, and then decrypts them, and handles
    them off to syscall(). This allows the network client to execute arbitrary
    syscalls in a safe way.
  </p>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">To give you an idea of what's needed:</p>

  <h2 class="text-[18pt] font-normal text-[#999] mt-6 mb-2">You'll need to</h2>

  <ol class="list-decimal list-inside pl-4 leading-[24px] mb-4">
    <li>
      If you are behind a NAT/PAT, you might want to do vortex level0, and
      complete this level from vortex, as otherwise your known IP address and
      port information changes.
    </li>
    <li>Examine how the RC4 keys are generated, and how they are applied</li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="http://www.linuxinfor.com/english/man2/mmap2.html"
        class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">mmap2()</a
      > some memory. You will get the address where the memory was allocated in the
      response from the server. mmap2() takes its arguments from registers, not the
      stack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="http://www.linuxinfor.com/english/man2/read.html"
        class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">read()</a
      > into that allocated buffer to get the file name. The source fd that you need
      will be 4. (0-2 = stdin/stdout/stderr, 3 will be network socket, so 4 in next
      in line. You will want to read in enough data for the filename "instructions"
    </li>
    <li>
      Write the filename ("instructions" sans quotes) to the allocated memory
      location to your client socket
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="http://www.linuxinfor.com/english/man2/open.html"
        class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">open()</a
      > the "instructions" file. You will get the fd it was allocated to in response
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="http://www.linuxinfor.com/english/man2/read.html"
        class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">read()</a
      > from the allocated file descriptor to your allocated memory
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="http://www.linuxinfor.com/english/man2/write.html"
        class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">write()</a
      > from the allocated buffer to the socket on the server (fd 4)
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    Once all that is done, you will have the contents of the instructions file
    printed to your screen.
  </p>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    Of course - this does not prevent you from using other mechanisms to access
    the server, such as using "shelldemo" from metasploit 2.x to examine the
    environment. In fact, using shelldemo is probably a good exercise as well.
  </p>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    In order to get the values for the parameters you need, you can use
    cross-references of linux code on the net, for example, to look up the value
    for __NR_read (linux read syscall()), we can use <a
      href="http://www.google.com.au/search?q=linux+lxr+__NR_read&ie=utf-8&oe=utf-8&aq=t&rls=org.mozilla:en-US:official&client=firefox-a"
      class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">this</a
    > which will lead us to <a
      href="http://lxr.linux.no"
      class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">http://lxr.linux.no</a
    >. From there we can use the search facility to find the values we need, <a
      href="http://lxr.linux.no/linux/include/asm-i386/unistd.h#L11"
      class="text-[#999] hover:text-[#CCC] no-underline">such as:</a
    >
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={readSyscallSnippet} />

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    If this level is too complicated / involved, please leave a comment. While
    it's complicated than adding several little endian integers together, I
    don't want the initial level to be overkill.
  </p>

  <p class="leading-[24px] mb-4">
    The following sourcecode is located in /drifter/drifter0_src/ :
  </p>

  <h3 class="text-[14pt] font-bold mt-4 mb-2">drifter0.c</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={drifter0Snippet} lang="c" />

  <h3 class="text-[14pt] font-bold mt-4 mb-2">rc4.c</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={rc4Snippet} lang="c" />

  <h3 class="text-[14pt] font-bold mt-4 mb-2">rc4.h</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={rc4hSnippet} lang="c" />
</GameLayout>
